WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:03.536
-You guys, we are thrilled
to be joined tonight

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.838
by the fantastic
cast and director

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:08.074
of the highly
anticipated film "Nope,"

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.076
which opens in theaters
and IMAX tomorrow.

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:13.746
Please welcome to the show
Daniel Kaluuya, Keke Palmer,

00:00:13.746 --> 00:00:16.316
Brandon Perea,
and Jordan Peele!

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.551
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:28.094 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.094 --> 00:00:37.637 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:40.105
Welcome, everybody.
-Hey.

00:00:40.105 --> 00:00:42.542
-Congratulations on the film.
It's fantastic.

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.444
Jordan, I just want to start.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:45.545
What does it feel like?

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:46.745
I know you had the premiere
on Monday.

00:00:46.745 --> 00:00:47.947
You get to see it with

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.248
a large group of people
for the first time.

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:50.583
This is an idea
that's obviously been

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.084
in your head for a long time.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:54.487
Is it very satisfying to see it
with a large group of people?

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:59.225
-It is very -- I love watching
these films with people.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.460
I love hearing an audience.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:03.596
And I also love it
when you don't hear an audience

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.197
and you look over
and they're like --

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.300
[ Laughter ]
That, I -- I love it.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.269
I get so much --
It's why I do it is

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:12.171
because I love this audience.

00:01:12.171 --> 00:01:16.842
And I'm just so -- I'm so proud
to be able to bring out

00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:19.112
three very sexy
people before me.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:20.346
-Stop.

00:01:20.346 --> 00:01:22.415
-So that it can...
[ Cheers ]

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:25.784
-...pave the -- it can sort of
lubricate the stage for me.

00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:29.722
But these guys, these guys
are so good in the movie

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.224
that it was just
a blast to shoot.

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:33.993
-Was that the first time,
Daniel,

00:01:33.993 --> 00:01:35.928
you had seen it
all the way through?

00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:37.129
-First time I'd seen it
finished, yeah.

00:01:37.129 --> 00:01:39.098
-Okay.
-I saw it in IMAX.

00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:41.400
It was way better in IMAX.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.804
It's like the sound and the --
it really helps

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.706
with the suspense and everything
that's happening here.

00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:49.141
-And of course, you know,
not to give anything away,

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:50.676
but there are effects
in the film.

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.078
Like, you were doing
a great deal

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.413
of acting with things
that were not there.

00:01:54.413 --> 00:01:55.415
-Yeah.
-Yes.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:57.450
It was two weeks
when I had nothing.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.118
[ Laughter ]
I would look at the sky.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:00.553
-You are a great --

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:04.090
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.026
-Jordan was like...

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:08.761
-Well, that must be
when you have to direct somebody

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.930
where you know what it's going
to look like and they don't.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:12.732
-Yeah. And you know, it's like
I'm talking to --

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:14.967
like, Daniel is
a brilliant thespian.

00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:16.636
He's Oscar Award-winning.

00:02:16.636 --> 00:02:18.571
So, I'm talking to --
and he's being --

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:21.073
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:21.073 --> 00:02:24.343
-You too, you too, man.
-Stop.

00:02:24.343 --> 00:02:26.612
My thespian days are over.

00:02:26.612 --> 00:02:31.017
No, but, look -- Daniel is a man
of few words, you know.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:33.152
But you just know
he's understanding

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:34.453
what you're putting down.

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:35.655
You know when
he's understanding.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:37.156
He's just, "Mm-hmm, yep, yep."

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:38.957
-That's so true.

00:02:38.957 --> 00:02:40.526
-And so, you have
this shorthand.

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:41.927
But when you have to be like
"And yeah and yeah,

00:02:41.927 --> 00:02:44.163
you're looking
at a flying saucer."

00:02:44.163 --> 00:02:46.766
And he's like,
"Hmm? Come again?"

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:49.468
You know, it's like, it really
is a whole different process

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:51.137
'cause he's used to looking
at somebody in the eyes

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:52.404
and talking to them.

00:02:52.404 --> 00:02:54.207
But, you know, the reason we --

00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.175
the reason he's so perfect
in this role

00:02:56.175 --> 00:02:57.910
is because, you know,

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.679
Daniel's eyes tell
so much of a --

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:01.915
so much of the story.

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:04.517
-So true.
-Appreciate that.

00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:07.854
Jordan's world,
he's made a unique world.

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:10.423
So, I felt like I understand
his language.

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.124
He's got his own
cinematic language.

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:15.661
And I relish kind of
like stepping into that

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:16.995
and actualizing that.
-Absolutely.

00:03:16.995 --> 00:03:18.897
-It would be very funny
if you went to the premiere

00:03:18.897 --> 00:03:20.165
and everything that he told you

00:03:20.165 --> 00:03:21.934
you were seeing
was the opposite.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.269
[ Laughter ]

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:27.306
If it's like a weird
sort of goofy animated deer.

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:29.708
-Daffy Duck.
-You're terrified.

00:03:29.708 --> 00:03:31.644
You're absolutely terrified.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:34.580
To the core.
-So, I know a lot of people,

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.448
obviously, Keke,
come out to this premiere.

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:37.716
I heard you met somebody,

00:03:37.716 --> 00:03:39.952
a celebrity at the premiere
you lost it for.

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:41.053
-Honey, I lost it.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.755
So, I grew up living
for "The Goonies."

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.023
-Sure.
-I literally have a tattoo

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:45.591
with my siblings,
"Goonies never say die."

00:03:45.591 --> 00:03:47.727
We literally live for that.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:50.629
Corey Feldman was
at the premiere, love.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:51.898
We wigged out!

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:54.900
I said, "Corey, my man Mouth
is in the building!"

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.502
I was so hyped.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.537
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.537 --> 00:03:59.571
-Now, I would imagine --
[ Applause ]

00:03:59.571 --> 00:04:02.875
Yeah, give it up
for Corey Feldman.

00:04:02.875 --> 00:04:05.911
I would imagine he was delighted
to have this response from you.

00:04:05.911 --> 00:04:07.513
Is that safe to say?
-Yes, but I feel like --

00:04:07.513 --> 00:04:09.915
I mean, he knows
"Goonies" is a classic.

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:12.652
I mean, the two Coreys,
rest in peace, Corey Haim.

00:04:12.652 --> 00:04:14.153
I mean, he knows.
So, he was just like --

00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:15.288
-He might know but I bet

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:16.789
he didn't go out
that night thinking

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:18.825
"I'm going to go get
some real 'Goonies' love."

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:21.026
[ Laughter ]

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:23.863
-But he is a legend for
our generation, as you know.

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:25.164
-Yes.
-And it is --

00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:26.865
there's a lot of sort of

00:04:26.865 --> 00:04:30.136
reference points
that align this movie

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:33.105
with like some of the big
sort of adventure movies

00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:34.506
from the '90s and '80s.

00:04:34.506 --> 00:04:41.246
So he was like -- it was key
we had Corey at our opening.

00:04:41.246 --> 00:04:42.882
-Jordan mentioned, of course,
that's what he lives for,

00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.216
seeing the audience reaction.

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:45.551
When you're watching this movie
for the first time,

00:04:45.551 --> 00:04:47.520
are any of you,
as the cast of the film,

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.922
turning to see the faces
of other people watching?

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.057
Are you curious --
-Always.

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:52.357
-Okay.

00:04:52.357 --> 00:04:54.227
-I'm that way even when
I'm not in the movie.

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:55.327
I love to see,
"What did you think?"

00:04:55.327 --> 00:04:56.963
And the funny thing --

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.431
the great thing about Jordan's
films is everybody

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:01.667
is always chewing
on a million things afterwards.

00:05:01.667 --> 00:05:02.869
So, me and my friends
are in debates,

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:04.036
like, "What did you think?"

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:05.604
"I don't know, did it happen?"
"What do you --"

00:05:05.604 --> 00:05:09.075
We always are trying to figure
out what he really was saying.

00:05:09.075 --> 00:05:11.244
-I can't handle it.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:12.945
-Oh, so you won't look
at the other faces?

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.148
-I won't.
And then I go toilet.

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:17.016
I always go to the bathroom.

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:18.684
For some reason, my bladder
goes, "We got to leave."

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:21.020
[ Laughter ]
-I know that feeling.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.756
-On the premiere,
I went toilet twice.

00:05:23.756 --> 00:05:26.225
I was like, "I'm done.
I can't.

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:27.993
I can't sit down."
I get stressed out.

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:30.662
I just -- you just --
You're so close to it.

00:05:30.662 --> 00:05:33.398
You're so close to it.
You're seeing all the details.

00:05:33.398 --> 00:05:34.934
You're feeling people react
to certain things.

00:05:34.934 --> 00:05:36.201
"Oh, I should have done that."

00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:37.236
It's a lot happening.

00:05:37.236 --> 00:05:39.572
-Now, I want to ask,
real quick,

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:41.774
you obviously haven't worked
with Jordan before.

00:05:41.774 --> 00:05:43.843
How did this come about
for you, Brandon?

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:47.547
-So, I was, I think,
the only person to audition for,

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:48.914
like, one of the
lead roles in the film.

00:05:48.914 --> 00:05:51.383
So, you get a Jordan Peele
audition, it's like,

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:53.085
"Oh, snap. Alright.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:55.154
It's about that time to like
gear things up."

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:57.089
And I was surprised
that he was auditioning

00:05:57.089 --> 00:06:00.859
someone for a lead role,
because he can get anyone

00:06:00.859 --> 00:06:02.361
he wants in the planet
I feel like.

00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:03.796
Everyone wants to work
with Jordan.

00:06:03.796 --> 00:06:05.765
So, I was just like, "Alright,
it's game time."

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:07.933
And it was cool because
I brought something different

00:06:07.933 --> 00:06:11.203
to the table than what the
script originally had planned.

00:06:11.203 --> 00:06:13.005
It was more of a quirky,
nerdy person.

00:06:13.005 --> 00:06:16.309
And I brought this edge
and hard, broken person

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:17.676
that's kind of over things.

00:06:17.676 --> 00:06:18.978
You know what I mean?

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:21.947
[ Applause ]
-By the way, you have no idea.

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:23.181
You know when you, like --

00:06:23.181 --> 00:06:25.284
you know when you see something
and you just,

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:28.554
for whatever reason,
it just hits your funny bone

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:29.988
and you just laugh
until you cry?

00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:32.224
It was one of these moments
we're in this audition,

00:06:32.224 --> 00:06:34.827
like he said, just came in with

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:38.697
just sort of a take
on the character.

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:40.032
And it just cracked me
up so much.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:42.067
I was like, "Dude."

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.604
I did one of those reality
show things where you're like,

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:47.707
"Well, I don't know
how it's going to go.

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:49.541
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.541 --> 00:06:51.844
"But you've got the job."

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:53.312
[ Laughter ]

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:57.349
And it was just -- it was just
one of these moments where --

00:06:57.349 --> 00:06:59.018
you know,
we just had this real bond.

00:06:59.018 --> 00:07:00.853
We were, like, we were
crying like babies.

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:02.121
-I cried.
You cried.

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:03.655
-It was
just one of these moments.

00:07:03.655 --> 00:07:05.658
And then the energy,
he came in --

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:07.826
I mean, watching these
three work together,

00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:10.262
for example is just one
of the joys of the film.

00:07:10.262 --> 00:07:13.899
Because their -- the balance is
just one of those great teams.

00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:15.901
And then of course you add
Michael Wincott,

00:07:15.901 --> 00:07:17.369
who's one
of my favorite actors.

00:07:17.369 --> 00:07:21.807
And Steven Yeun who just
takes on this really --

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:24.410
-It is a wonder,
because, you know, for me,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.111
one of the wonderful things
about this film

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:30.015
is it is about people to
some degree working together.

00:07:30.015 --> 00:07:32.851
And it came at a time where
people weren't working together

00:07:32.851 --> 00:07:34.186
because of the pandemic.

00:07:34.186 --> 00:07:36.755
And so, it was so great to see
actors together again

00:07:36.755 --> 00:07:39.925
and see obviously everything
behind making a film.

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:42.394
You had, obviously,
worked with Jordan before.

00:07:42.394 --> 00:07:43.729
How does he approach you?

00:07:43.729 --> 00:07:45.130
You know, after the success
of "Get Out,"

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:47.266
does he have to sell much
when he tells you about "Nope"

00:07:47.266 --> 00:07:50.303
or --
-He doesn't have to but he did.

00:07:50.303 --> 00:07:53.105
[ Laughter ]

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:56.542
He spoke to me about a film
a while ago, and it went quiet.

00:07:56.542 --> 00:07:58.077
And then he came back.
It was at pandemic,

00:07:58.077 --> 00:08:00.546
so I was just like
in the house by myself.

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:02.047
And then Jordan Peele's
calling me.

00:08:02.047 --> 00:08:04.449
I'm like, "Pick up."

00:08:04.449 --> 00:08:08.754
And then he spoke about
the film, like, scene for scene,

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:10.022
beat for beat over the phone.

00:08:10.022 --> 00:08:11.991
It's like a two hour
and a half phone call.

00:08:11.991 --> 00:08:13.392
And he's like,
"What did you think?"

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:16.228
I'm like, "I'm going
to have to process that."

00:08:16.228 --> 00:08:17.662
When you watch the film,
you'd understand.

00:08:17.662 --> 00:08:20.199
It's this and it's that
and it's this and it's that.

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:22.301
Yeah, a lot's happening, man,
a lot's happening, man.

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:27.273
For me, it was just a -- For me,
it was ambitious and audacious.

00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:28.340
That's what I loved about it.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.542
It was just,
"Oh, he's going for it."

00:08:29.542 --> 00:08:30.909
I mean, and so,
I just respected that,

00:08:30.909 --> 00:08:33.279
and I like to do that too.

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:35.981
So, I always like it.
-You're a fan of horror,

00:08:35.981 --> 00:08:39.051
and you actually reached out
to Jordan in the past.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:40.252
-Yes.
-And this really paid off

00:08:40.252 --> 00:08:41.721
then, obviously,
because here you are.

00:08:41.721 --> 00:08:43.055
-You know, I never
asked you, Jordan,

00:08:43.055 --> 00:08:44.690
because I don't even know
if you look at your DMs.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.091
Did you even see
when I DM'd you?

00:08:46.091 --> 00:08:47.292
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:47.292 --> 00:08:49.361
-Obviously,
Jordan doesn't look at his DMs.

00:08:49.361 --> 00:08:51.730
But I DM'd you, man,
right after "Get Out." I said,

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:55.000
"Dude, we worked together
on 'Key &amp; Peele.'"

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.070
-So, after we had been
working together

00:08:58.070 --> 00:09:01.006
for a couple of months,
you brought it up.

00:09:01.006 --> 00:09:02.007
And I think you brought it up

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:03.475
thinking
somehow that was a thing.

00:09:03.475 --> 00:09:05.244
You were like, "You know, when
I DM'd you

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:06.879
a couple years ago
and you responded."

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:10.015
I look, and all of a sudden,
I see this DM from legendary,

00:09:10.015 --> 00:09:12.251
iconic Keke Palmer, by the way.
-Stop!

00:09:12.251 --> 00:09:15.354
-I would never --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:15.354 --> 00:09:18.690
I would never, like -- I would
never disrespect her like that.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.826
But I legit didn't see it.

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:23.595
And so, we're working together,
and I see this --

00:09:23.595 --> 00:09:25.765
"Let's work together
some day" moment.

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:28.934
And I'm like, "Keke, look!"

00:09:28.934 --> 00:09:30.302
It didn't even take that.

00:09:30.302 --> 00:09:31.770
I loved you anyway.
-I love you, J.P.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:34.907
-You should have responded then
while you were on set

00:09:34.907 --> 00:09:36.308
and be like,
"Great, let's do it."

00:09:36.308 --> 00:09:37.442
[ Laughter ]

00:09:37.442 --> 00:09:38.644
And it'd be like,
"Something's weird.

00:09:38.644 --> 00:09:40.512
I think my DMs are just
going through now."

00:09:40.512 --> 00:09:43.782
[ Laughter ]
I have a million more questions

00:09:43.782 --> 00:09:45.116
to ask you guys
about the movies.

00:09:45.116 --> 00:09:48.220
We will be right back with more
from the cast of "Nope."

